            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for to swindle, then the French for the fraud."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: l'agression",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "l'agression — \"the attack, the assault\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Une agression a eu lieu près du marché. An assault took place near the market."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "An attack on a person. A hostile mood is l'agressivité."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "agression is a learned borrowing from Latin aggressiō \"a going towards\", from aggredī \"to approach, to attack\", built on gradī \"to step\" (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
